SSDFS tools: VM tests
=====================

These scripts are executed inside a virtual machine (as root) with
SSDFS kernel driver and a disposable block device. They are NOT part
of "make check": they destroy the content of the device.

Every guest script has the same interface:

    <script> <device> <log dir>

    Environment:
      TOOLS_DIR - ssdfs-tools build (default: this source tree,
                  i.e. the tools are built in-tree)
      MKFS_OPTS - options of mkfs.ssdfs

Scripts
-------

guest-fsck.sh
    mkfs -> fsck -> mount, I/O, umount -> fsck -> recoverfs.
    Checks that fsck.ssdfs reports healthy volume (exit code 0)
    and recoverfs.ssdfs succeeds on the volume after I/O.
    FSCK_OPTS defines fsck options (default: -n -f -v).
    Debug output: <log dir>/fsck-{fresh,after-io}-debug.log,
                  <log dir>/recoverfs-after-io-debug.log.

guest-fsck-corrupt.sh
    fsck.ssdfs/recoverfs.ssdfs corruption tests. The volume is
    created once by mkfs.ssdfs. Every test case restores pristine
    metadata (the first 128MB), corrupts one field of the base
    snapshot segment's log (PEB #0) by ssdfs-corrupt.py and checks:
      (1) exit code of fsck.ssdfs;
      (2) diagnostic message of the corrupted item (debug output);
      (3) number of corrupted items of base snapshot segment.
    Also checks fsck exit codes of usage error and absent volume,
    that fsck.ssdfs reports no SSDFS volume (without suggesting
    the recovery) if ext4 has been created over SSDFS volume
    (requires mkfs.ext4 in the guest), and that recoverfs.ssdfs
    finds a valid PEB if PEB #0 is corrupted. The zeroed-first-4kb
    case zeroes the segment header of PEB #0: only base snapshot
    segment has to be reported as not found (the rest of metadata
    is located by volume header of another PEB). The zeroed-head case
    re-creates the volume with 128KB erase size, copies PEB #8 into
    PEB #313 and zeroes PEBs #0-312: fsck.ssdfs has to scan the whole
    device and report the corrupted volume, recoverfs.ssdfs and
    dump.ssdfs (-g) have to find the valid PEB.
    CASES selects test cases (default: all).
    Output: CORRUPT-TEST <case> PASS|FAIL lines, the final
            "SSDFS-TEST: PASS|FAIL" line, <log dir>/corrupt/<case>.log.
    Requires python3 in the guest.

ssdfs-corrupt.py <device> <case>
ssdfs-corrupt.py --list
    Corrupts one field of segment header or log footer of PEB #0
    (header-segbmap-* and header-maptbl-* cases corrupt segment bitmap
    and mapping table headers of the volume header, including their
    metadata extents). The checksum of the modified structure is
    recalculated (except
    signature and checksum cases), so only the target item has to be
    detected as corrupted. The exception is header-log-* and
    header-pebs-per-seg cases: the volume geometry fields depend on
    each other, so all items that use the geometry are detected as
    corrupted. fsck.ssdfs takes the volume geometry from PEB #0 if
    the geometry looks sane (header-log-segsize case), otherwise
    from another PEB (header-log-erasesize case). The
    header-log-erasesize-max and header-log-segsize-overflow cases
    check extreme values (fsck.ssdfs must not fail or crash).
    The layout offsets correspond to
    include/ssdfs_abi.h: update them if on-disk layout changes.

fsck.ssdfs corruption report (-R|--report-level)
-------------------------------------------------

    1|summary  - state of key metadata structures (default)
    2|parts    - + corrupted parts of metadata structures
                 with the number of corrupted items
    3|full     - + every corrupted item

guest-fsck-corrupt.sh uses -R 3 to check the reported items.

fsck.ssdfs exit codes
---------------------

    0  - no errors
    1  - errors corrected
    4  - errors left uncorrected
    8  - operational error (including absent SSDFS volume)
    16 - usage error

Example (virtme-ng)
-------------------

    ./autogen.sh && ./configure && make
    truncate -s 2G /tmp/ssdfs.img
    mkdir -p logs
    vng --run <kernel build> --disk /tmp/ssdfs.img --user root \
        --rwdir $PWD --exec \
        "tests/vm/guest-fsck-corrupt.sh /dev/vda $PWD/logs"
